{
  "markdown_report": "At 0% progress, prioritize **controlled random-prefix truncation training for NextItNet**, supported by padding and history-sensitivity diagnostics. This is a proposed experiment; no improvement has been measured.\n\n**Starting-point insights.** (1) Feedback implicates broad relevance and stale interests; recent-context reliance is a hypothesis, not established intent. (2) Training-pair margins depend on score scale and cannot establish ranking improvements. (3) Left padding requires final-nonpadding readout, never count−1; intermediate padding behavior also needs verification.\n\n**Related-work insights.** (1) NextItNet’s causal convolutions provide a calculable receptive field [1]. Count every temporal convolution: R=1+Σ(k−1)d; the supplied schedule remains unverified. (2) CL4SRec already uses cropping [4], limiting novelty; DuoRec and EC4SRec warn that augmentation can disrupt semantics [5,6]. (3) Shuffling motivates behavioral controls [2], while sampled-metric bias limits conclusions to the pinned benchmark [3].\n\n**Framework and directions.** Implementation correctness → padding/readout audits; temporal reliance → prefix interventions; supervision effects → matched-mask controls; predictive usefulness → fixed-candidate validation with popularity/history-length strata. Sensitivity alone establishes neither beneficial personalization nor causal intent.\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality/potential/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n| Idea | Algorithm and reference | Scores | Positive / negative assessment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Padding-safe residual blocks | Audit leading-padding invariance; mask padding after temporal blocks if needed [1,7]. | 2/7/2 | Originality: targeted adaptation / established technique. Potential: dependable foundation / gains require a defect. Difficulty: local edits / predictor must match. |\n| Controlled random-prefix truncation | Occasionally remove older context; compare against identical target masks without removal [1,4–6]. | 2/6/4 | Originality: NextItNet-specific controlled study / cropping is established. Potential: inexpensive robustness / useful long-term context may disappear. Difficulty: small transformation / controls require careful alignment. |\n| Learned dilation gates | Jointly train scalar residual gates [1]. | 4/6/5 | Originality: adaptive context combination / familiar gating. Potential: flexible span / gates may collapse. Difficulty: modest architecture change / predictor and checkpoints change. |\n\n**Selected experiment.** Retain the inexpensive truncation idea, but isolate context removal from supervision changes. Predeclare probability 0.25 and uniformly sampled retained lengths 8–32, clipped to available history; these remain unvalidated hypotheses. Report actual retained lengths, supervised-target counts, and their relation to R.\n\n```text\nA: intact inputs; baseline valid-target loss\nB: intact inputs; sampled suffix target mask\nC: same inputs, targets, negatives and suffix mask as B;\n   additionally zero older input positions without shifting\nretain original successors and immediate predecessors\noptimize the same loss, normalized by valid-target count\n```\n\nA→B measures supervision redistribution; B→C isolates context removal. Match sampled users, target identities, negatives and optimizer steps between B/C. Separately compare all-position and final-position loss under identical contexts if budget permits. Preserve causal inputs; targets must never enter their own receptive fields.\n\nEvaluate intact histories with identical pinned candidates. Select solely by macro validation HR@10; report category scores and paired user-bootstrap macro differences [8]. One extra hit changes category HR by 0.005 and macro HR by 0.00125. Bootstrap intervals exclude adaptive-selection, training-seed and candidate-sampling uncertainty [9]. Log every attempt and predeclare the affordable comparison budget.\n\nRetain one model/checkpoint per category, seed 42, at most 200 epochs, 200 batches/epoch, preprocessing seed 0, routing, splits and DATA_DIR. Preserve both diagnostic APIs, ModelView, trial-local artifacts and embedded-predictor consistency. Report valid counts and unavailable measurements explicitly. Cropping may encourage short-context shortcuts; intact-versus-perturbed validation comparisons and history-length strata help detect this, without changing the evaluator.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Evaluate random-prefix truncation within the supplied NextItNet using matched supervision controls. Train exactly one independent model per category in each experimental condition; compare conditions without combining their predictions or checkpoints.",
    "motivation": "Qualitative feedback suggests older or isolated interactions may distract from coherent recent behavior, but does not establish current intent or satisfied needs. Cropping has established precedents, so the contribution is a controlled test of whether removing older accessible context improves this benchmark beyond changes in target weighting or supervision volume.",
    "implementation_notes": "Use three predeclared conditions: A uses intact inputs and baseline supervision; B uses intact inputs with sampled suffix target masks; C uses the same masks as B and additionally removes older input context. In B/C, independently select rows with probability 0.25 and uniformly draw an integer retained length from 8 through 32, clipped to available nonpadding input length. For selected rows, retain original positions at or after the resulting cutoff; do not compact or shift. Preserve each retained target's original next-item alignment and immediate predecessor. Intersect the suffix mask with baseline valid-target positions. Generate B/C masks through a dedicated seeded augmentation RNG so augmentation does not change user, target or negative sampling; use matching initial weights, batch streams and optimizer steps. Use the identical baseline loss formula with explicit valid-target normalization; B/C must have identical denominators. Skip empty supervised batches consistently. A/B comparisons measure the combined effect of reduced and redistributed supervision, not a pure target-weighting effect. Each condition trains its own models from the supplied cold start; no checkpoints from other trials are reused, and no conditions are fused at inference. Bound this study by the remaining shared 43,200 GPU-second budget, counting controls, diagnostics and failures; defer optional all-position versus final-position comparisons if unaffordable. Before interpreting cropping, verify the baseline's actual dilation schedule, temporal convolutions, normalization axes, causality and padding behavior. Compute R = 1 + sum((kernel_width - 1) * dilation) over every stride-one temporal convolution; this describes accessible context only when other operations do not mix time globally. Leading-padding invariance requires preserving all real interactions and disabling dropout. For equal-length prefix interventions, changes strictly outside the receptive field should leave final features unchanged under position-local operations. Nonzero padded intermediate states can invalidate assumptions about replacing real prefixes with padding; audit them rather than assuming zero embeddings suffice. If padding fixes are necessary, apply them consistently to all conditions and update the embedded predictor. Evaluate intact left-padded histories at their final nonpadding position, normally L-1; never use nonpadding_count-1. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), and all other host ModelView interfaces. Do not invent unavailable categories, reviews, compatibility labels or user traits. Report undefined diagnostics as unavailable, with valid counts. Keep four-category routing, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, at most 200 epochs and 200 batches per epoch. Preserve splits, negative candidates, evaluator and checkpoint/predictor consistency. Select only by the prescribed macro validation HR@10. If the host exposes per-user validation hits, bootstrap paired user indices within each category and average four category differences equally; otherwise do not claim an interval. Predeclare configuration and checkpoint-selection rules and log every attempted condition. Intervals for selected winners do not correct validation-selection bias. Monitor history-length and training-popularity strata and intact-versus-perturbed behavior for short-context shortcuts. These remain exploratory diagnostics, not evidence of ownership, replacement needs or causal intent.",
    "pseudocode": "conditions = ['A_full', 'B_mask_only', 'C_crop_and_mask']\n# Run conditions separately from the supplied cold start; never fuse models.\nfor condition in conditions:\n    reset_training_seed(42)\n    model, optimizer = supplied_nextitnet_cold_start()\n    batch_rng = dedicated_baseline_rng(seed=42)\n    augmentation_rng = dedicated_augmentation_rng(seed=42)\n    for step in prescribed_training_steps:\n        sequence, positive, negative = baseline_sample_batch(batch_rng)\n        valid = baseline_valid_target_mask(sequence, positive)\n        suffix_mask = ones_like(valid)\n        cropped_sequence = sequence.clone()\n        # Generate the same augmentation schedule in every condition.\n        for b in rows(sequence):\n            occupied = nonzero_positions(sequence[b])\n            crop_row = augmentation_rng.uniform() < 0.25\n            if crop_row and len(occupied) > 0:\n                s = min(augmentation_rng.randint_inclusive(8, 32), len(occupied))\n                cutoff = occupied[-s]\n                suffix_mask[b, :cutoff] = False\n                cropped_sequence[b, :cutoff] = 0\n        inputs = cropped_sequence if condition == 'C_crop_and_mask' else sequence\n        supervised = valid if condition == 'A_full' else (valid & suffix_mask)\n        if not supervised.any():\n            continue\n        features = model.log2feats(inputs)\n        loss = baseline_loss_with_explicit_valid_normalization(\n            features, positive, negative, supervised)\n        optimizer.zero_grad()\n        loss.backward()\n        optimizer.step()\n# B and C share target identities, negatives, masks and loss denominators.\n# Evaluate every condition with intact histories and final-nonpadding readout.\n# Host selection uses unchanged four-category macro validation HR@10.\n# Each category predictor loads exactly one trained model/checkpoint.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 2,
      "positive": "The matched-mask comparison gives a precise NextItNet-specific test separating context removal from altered supervision.",
      "negative": "Random cropping and subsequence training have clear precedents; neither cropping nor the proposed controls establish a novel model class."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "A controlled finding could motivate context selection inside one jointly trained NextItNet and clarify when older history helps.",
      "negative": "Benefits may be benchmark-specific regularization; random removal can discard useful semantics and encourage excessive reliance on recent items."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The intervention is a small batch transformation and adds no inference component.",
      "negative": "Reproducible matched sampling, loss normalization, padding audits and multiple training controls require more work and GPU budget than the original sampler-only description suggested."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
